 ratings."]}, "overview": "Ohio starts as a Republican-leaning presidential state with the incumbent president term-limited and no successor advantage on either side. The state's partisan lean of roughly five points toward Republicans anchors the outlook, and with no general-election polls or expert ratings yet published, there is little hard evidence to move from that starting point. Early 2028 primary attention centers on home-state figures like Vance and Ramaswamy, and a home-state nominee could push Ohio further right.\n\nDemocratic optimism is visible in state party fundraising circles, and national headlines describe a sharp swing against Trump-aligned Republicans in some states, but neither signal is specific or strong enough to reposition Ohio. The race rates Lean Republican, with wide uncertainty until polling and ratings arrive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2797, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3055, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.299, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3175, "margin": -5.0}]}